<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kano</h1><p class="st-meta">999 CE – 1805 CE (approx.) · capital Kano</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest Hausa kingdom, a walled market city whose dyed cloth and leather were traded across the Sahara and the Sudan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> dates the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagauda">Bagauda</a>, said to have become king in 999 among communities of farmers and ironworkers around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dala-hill">Dala hill</a>. Over the following centuries the rulers extended their authority over the surrounding plain and built the earthen walls that enclosed farmland as well as the city, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> defensible in long sieges.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was adopted at court under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-yaji">Ali Yaji</a> in the 14th century, brought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Wangara</a> traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a>, ruling in the late 15th century, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa">Gidan Rumfa</a> palace and the Kurmi market and took advice from the scholar al-Maghili on Islamic government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> prospered on textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> dyeing, leather and the caravan trade, at times paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> reformers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> drove out the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-alwali-ii">Muhammad Alwali II</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kano-emirate">Kano Emirate</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. The city remained the commercial heart of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and its emir still reigns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 999 CE – 1805 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:79.58%;width:16.04%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>999 CE – 1805 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kano</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bagauda, Ali Yaji, Muhammad Rumfa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nigeria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kano Sultanate, Kano</dd></dl></aside></div>
